  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nWe continue to see a 600 dam H5 ridge slowly retrograding across the \nLower Mississippi Valley area towards the Southern Plains keeping \nhot and mostly subsident airmass over the area through the remainder \nof the work week. This feature represents an extreme event \ngiven NAEFS standardized anomalies of 3-4. This will keep our \narea well above average for late Sept as we move into the \nbeginning of the weekend. Meanwhile, a robust mid/upper trough \nover the Pacific Northwest is progged to move across the \nNorthern Intermountain Region on Friday before arriving over the\nCentral and Northern Rockies as we move into Saturday. A lead \nshortwave trough is progged to race eastward across the \nCentral/Northern Plains helping to drive a cold front southward \nacross the Central High Plains on Saturday. This front is \nexpected to arrive in our forecast area by late afternoon or \nearly evening providing a focus for showers and thunderstorms. A\nline of thunderstorms appears most likely although other more \nisolated activity could develop along the dryline late in the \nday or during the evening hours. Some severe storms may develop \ngiven steepening mid-lvl lapse rates, moderate CAPE and deep \nlayer shear in excess of 30 knots. Showers and thunderstorms may\nlinger in the post-frontal airmass with a continued threat for \nsevere storms Saturday night. The threat for strong or severe \nstorms may move south of the area as we move into Sunday as a \nmore stable post-frontal airmass arrives.\n\nMon-Thu...a stable  post-frontal regime will build across the area \nas we move into early next week with seasonable temperatures \nexpected. Another shortwave trough may impact the area in the Wed-\nThu timeframe bringing increasing probabilities for showers and \nstorms.",
